Why do so few monsters follow you up and down stairs?  It seems to me 
that it wouldn't take much intelligence to see that your prey is 
escaping, and that there's an easy way to pursue it.  In fact, stopping 
at the stairs seems UNnatural for mindless predators like gelatinous 
cubes.

It makes good sense that some monsters are simply not anatomically 
equipped (e.g. grid bugs, many quadrupeds... I'm having trouble thinking 
of good examples), and others may be content with simply chasing you 
away from their stomping grounds (e.g., many intelligent humanoids).  
But why would, say, an angry bee let you get away?

Is this a balance issue?  I guess wide-open levels like the Mines would 
become more dangerous, but I'm inclined to say that this would be a good 
thing.

Or is it a coding issue?  Does each monster have a "will pursue across 
levels" attribute, or is it based on their type, or what?

Vaguely related: Why are there so few ladders?  (IIRC, only Vlad's and 
Rodney's towers have them.)  Is there a different subset of monsters who 
will pursue you up or down a ladder?

If so, perhaps some stairs in the main dungeon could be randomly 
converted to ladders.  This would introduce a bit more strategy when 
using them as emergency exits.

